<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-malaria">
  <title>Comparing tests for malaria</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>"There are more people dying of malaria than any specific cancer."</p>
      <p>--- Bill Gates</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      This chapter examines data from an evaluation of a new magneto-optical malaria test (ROMD) against three
      established methods (PCR, RDT, LM).
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Questions.</em> How much do results from the four tests agree, and on which cases do they differ?
    </p>
    <p><em>Sources.</em> Arndt et al. malaria testing study data from Papua New Guinea.</p>
    <p><em>Structure.</em> 956 observations with 24 variables.</p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-malaria-agreement">
    <title>How much agreement is there between the four tests?</title>

    <p>
      Cases with missing values for key tests were excluded before comparing outcomes. The four methods produce
      different positive rates, with ROMD reporting the highest proportion of positives.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-maltest">
      <image source="images/malTest-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Percentages of positives for the four malaria tests.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      A matrix-style display of result combinations shows all possible four-test positive/negative patterns and how
      often each occurs.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-malvis">
      <image source="images/malvis-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Combinations of individual malaria test results (red rectangles are positives).</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Paired comparisons confirm that no single test strictly dominates another: disagreements occur in both
      directions for each pair.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-malpcp">
      <image source="images/malpcp-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Paired comparisons of the binary results of the four tests.</p></caption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-malaria-additional">
    <title>Additional analyses</title>

    <p>
      Treating LM as a reference, the ROMD magneto-optical measurement distribution can be compared for LM-negative
      and LM-positive groups on a log scale.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-mallm">
      <image source="images/malLM-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>Magneto-optical measurements on a log10 scale grouped by corresponding LM test result.</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Overlap between groups indicates that cut-off choice affects false-positive and false-negative trade-offs.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-malaw">
      <image source="images/malAW-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Weight by age and sex of people tested for malaria.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Age-weight scatterplots provide context on the tested population and highlight a few unusual observations.
    </p>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        <em>Answers.</em> The methods agree in many cases but disagree in roughly one third; the proposed ROMD method
        classifies more cases as positive than the other three tests.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Further questions.</em> How sensitive are conclusions to the ROMD cut-off choice? Do additional variables
        help explain cases where methods disagree?
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-malaria-takeaways">
      <title>Graphical takeaways</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Matrix displays effectively summarize multivariate categorical outcomes.</p></li>
        <li><p>Quantitative test distributions make cut-off consequences directly visible.</p></li>
        <li><p>Scatterplots provide fast context for relationships in supporting variables.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
